meta {
  name: Write a match grid by hand
  type: http
  seq: 7
}

post {
  url: {{baseUrl}}/api/v1/assessments/{{assessmentId}}/questions
  body: json
  auth: inherit
}

body:json {
  {
    "format": "match",
    "stem": "Match each chloroplast structure to what happens there.",
    "prompt_items": [
      { "key": "1", "text": "Stroma" },
      { "key": "2", "text": "Thylakoid membrane" },
      { "key": "3", "text": "Outer membrane" }
    ],
    "options": [
      { "key": "A", "text": "The light-independent reactions" },
      { "key": "B", "text": "The light-dependent reactions" },
      { "key": "C", "text": "Separates the organelle from the cytosol" }
    ],
    "answer_key": {
      "pairs": { "1": "A", "2": "B", "3": "C" }
    },
    "points": 3,
    "difficulty": "understand"
  }
}

script:post-response {
  bru.setEnvVar("matchQuestionId", res.body.id);
}

assert {
  res.status: eq 201
  res.body.type: eq match
  res.body.format: eq match
  res.body.origin: eq written
}

docs {
  The structured half of the format taxonomy, exercised by hand.

  **`type` is not a field.** It is the grading family, and the service derives it from
  `format` — fourteen formats onto six marking paths. Letting a client send both would
  allow a question drawn as a match grid and marked as a multiple choice, which scores
  zero for everybody who sat it while looking completely normal on screen. Postgres
  holds the same mapping as a check constraint, so a bug here fails the INSERT rather
  than the exam.

  **The keys you send are not the keys you get back.** The service renumbers the left
  column onto `1..n` and the right onto `A..H`, and remaps `pairs` in the same pass —
  models key options `"a"`, `"1"` or repeat one, and renumbering without remapping the
  answer marks the paper against the wrong letters. Send whatever you like; read the
  response to find out what it became.

  **`answer_key` never reaches a sitter.** It is one column rather than four
  (`correct_options`, `accepted`, `pairs`, `order` all live in it) because the rule that
  matters is that it is ABSENT from `public.question_sit`. One column is one thing to
  keep out of one view.

  Three pairs, three marks: each independently marked part is worth one, so a sitter who
  gets two of the three scores two rather than nothing.
}
